  "record": {
    "read_status": "partial",
    "file_problem": "The article text is present, but its test-accuracy results are referred to without the graph or numerical values. The text says only that sparse encodings consistently under-perform the original hidden representations.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T5",
    "tranche_reason": "It tests whether pretrained SAE encodings retain information useful for sentiment classification, using logistic-regression accuracy rather than the original toy-model superposition method.",
    "gate_note": "The article reports a qualitative test-accuracy comparison for two Pythia models, but the supplied text contains no accuracy values or layer-by-layer accuracy results. It also gives layer-specific SAE reconstruction MSE and cosine similarities; these measure reconstruction fidelity, not whether dictionary features are recovered as features, so those rows are linked to none of the claim versions. Commenters' observations are not treated as the article's results.",
    "test_reason": "It compares logistic-regression sentiment accuracy on pretrained SAE encodings against the raw Pythia hidden states. Sparse encodings 'consistently under-perform', so the SAE features lose task-relevant information the model holds. That bears on whether dictionary features are a complete account of the representation (V5). It does not test whether sparse features exist or are recoverable (V4).",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The author reports that sparse encodings are significantly and consistently worse for sentiment classification than raw hidden states. She is unsure whether high-level features are poorly encoded or she made a mistake, and so did not pursue steering or removal.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses pretrained SAEs on Pythia hidden states and compares logistic-regression sentiment-classification accuracy; it does not reproduce the original toy-network superposition experiments.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The author says results were available for only a few layers because the same configuration could not be loaded consistently for all layers. Layer-specific classification outcomes are not included in the supplied text.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Reconstruction fidelity of pretrained SAEs across layers. Cosine similarity is about 0.80–0.95; MSE is 0.03–0.27, rising to 14.6 at the 410m final layer (23).",
        "proposed_claim": "Pretrained SAE reconstruction fidelity on Pythia varies by layer and degrades sharply at the final layer.",
